  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Addressing the 404**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the 404 error. It\'s clear that the `/api/chat` route will bomb out on GitHub Pages due to static hosting restrictions. I\'m focusing on how `AIChatbot.tsx`\'s catch block can handle this. The aim is to ensure a graceful degradation, or to find a more elegant solution. This is great context!\n\n\n**Refining the Error Handling**\n\nI\'ve got a fantastic improvement! The chatbot error handling now gracefully adapts based on the environment. I\'m modifying the catch block in `AIChatbot.tsx` to detect if the app is on GitHub Pages, using `window.location.hostname`. If so, it provides a friendly, static-site-specific message with Vetrivel\'s contact details, perfectly addressing the 404 issue in this context.\n\n\n**Investigating the Root Cause**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into that 404. It\'s clear that the deployment issue isn\'t just about the `/api/chat` route, but also includes that problematic `/src/main.tsx` request, which should not have happened. I suspect that the raw source code has been mistakenly served on GitHub Pages, likely due to a misconfiguration or lack of a build step during the deployment process. That\'s the key problem to address!\n\n\n**Implementing the Workflow**\n\nI\'m now implementing the complete fix. It appears the main source of this 404 is the incorrect deployment of raw source files. The key is in the build process: I\'m implementing a GitHub Actions workflow to ensure the `/dist` folder is deployed, and to configure `vite.config.ts` to use relative paths. This ensures the correct built files are served, resolving the 404.\n\n\n**Designing the Deployment Workflow**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the deployment workflow design. I\'ve decided to leverage `actions/deploy-pages`, the modern GitHub Pages deployment method, to sidestep the need for a separate `gh-pages` branch. The plan involves a `.github/workflows/deploy.yml` that configures necessary permissions (`pages: write`, `id-token: write`). I\'m also ensuring the workflow triggers on both `main` and `master` branches for maximum compatibility, providing a robust and safe deployment process!\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will modify `vite.config.ts` to add `base: \'./\'`. This makes all compiled asset paths relative rather than absolute. In a static deployment like GitHub Pages, absolute paths (such as `/assets/index.js`) resolve to the root domain (`https://vetrivelm02.github.io/assets/index.js`) and fail with 404s when the repository is hosted in a subfolder like `/vetrivel-portfolio/`. Setting `base: \'./\'` ensures that all built assets are resolved correctly."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 7
      nanos: 327615200
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
